\DOC LOCAL_AMONTSQR_P256_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_AMONTSQR_P256_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-execution goal past an inlined
almost-Montgomery-squaring subroutine, replacing the current symbolic state
with a successor state and recording the squaring result as new assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which variant/entry of the subroutine
block to step over; the second is a {string list} naming the output and input
quantities, the first name being used for the result value and the second for
the squared input, thereby fixing the names appearing in the new assumptions.
The resulting single subgoal keeps the same {eventually} postcondition but
applies it to a fresh successor state variable in place of the previous one.
Assumptions mentioning the old state — the program-counter value,
{bytes_loaded}, register reads, memory-content equations, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} frame from the initial state — are discharged and re-added restated
for the new state, with the program counter advanced, the {MAYCHANGE} frame
extended by the newly written memory component, and an extra memory-content
equation giving the result value at the output location. Two further arithmetic
facts are added: one bounding the result below {2 EXP 256}, and one asserting
it is congruent to the Montgomery-scaled square of the input. Pre-existing side
conditions that do not refer to the state, such as nonoverlapping conditions
and earlier arithmetic facts, are retained, and an initial trivial {MAYCHANGE}
reflexivity assumption, when present, is dropped.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single transformation, on goals of this
machine-state form.

\ENDDOC
